  "result": "The O3 lesson is written, with a short quiz and a cheat sheet. I haven't opened the lesson in a browser. I only wrote the HTML, so the interactive checks are untested. I wrote my own quiz answers from the stems alone, but no solver check has run.\n\n- **Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`, 30 minutes. By the end, you can decide whether each piece of information in the makerspace assistant belongs in the context, in saved notes, or in a library it searches. It covers:\n  - the context window and context rot;\n  - what carries between steps: the program re-sends the whole context on every call, so the cost grows;\n  - notes between sessions, including the memory tool;\n  - retrieval of manual chunks.\n\n  It has two figures, a token-cost worked example with a faded \"your turn\", and practice checks with instant feedback.\n- **Quiz:** `quizzes/0002.json` with its key `keys/0002.json`. It has five new items, none repeated from the lesson's practice. Ask me when you want to take it.\n- **Also written:** `cheatsheets/context-and-memory.html`, `RESOURCES.md`, and a new line in `NOTES.md`.\n\nI built the lesson on Anthropic's context-engineering post and the Claude docs on context windows and the memory tool. I read all three through a fetch helper, which gave me summaries rather than the full pages. I took the retrieval and chunking material from the abstract-level search result for a research paper, which I didn't read in full. I found no online community I could vouch for, so the lesson points you to your makerspace staff and design review instead.\n\nQuiz 0001 is still ungraded (`submissions/s1.json` and `s2.json`). Want me to grade both attempts next?",
